//
//   - key row in the business transaction (Postgres, pgx): Middleware = the real constructor over
//     a testcontainers pool; Write = an INSERT through the tx bound into ctx by the repo's
//     TxBinder / unit-of-work join, into a scratch table the test creates; Committed = a COUNT on
//     the pool (a different connection, so only committed rows count); KeyRows = a COUNT on the
//     idempotency table. Releasing = rollback, so KeyRows 0 and Committed 0 are real proofs.
//   - savepoint design: the same as above; the handler runs behind a SAVEPOINT, still one
//     transaction, nothing else changes in the wiring.
//   - key row written separately from the business transaction (a Store behind a port): Write goes
//     through the repo's unit of work the way a use case does; Committed counts after the request
//     finished; KeyRows asks the store port (an in-memory fake of the port is enough, if it is the
//     same fake the real middleware tests use). Releasing = the port's Delete/Release call.
//
// Refuse must call the production error path (writeError / WriteProblem) with the entry's error or
// slug, so the production class marking runs. Do NOT write the response by hand here.
